-- §6  What the fixtures decline to answer, which is the whole reason they exist.
# One row per (question, reason) the fixtures file, with how many times.
#
# Reports about the world must not count stipulations
#   This is the other half of that rule. `rules.sql` composes reports/absences_in_the_corpus.sqlc
#   and correctly does not compose this one: a fixture asserting independence on purpose would
#   answer "has anybody tested the assumption?" with a yes nobody earned. But the fixture census
#   is still worth reading. It is just not a finding, so it belongs in an observation and not in a
#   report of record.
#
# This section is its caller
#   That is the point of the section. A relation that composes, emits, and is read by nothing
#   reads from the outside exactly like a relation that does not exist, which is what
#   examples/observations/main.rs refuses to allow.
-- reports/absences_in_the_fixtures.sqlc, given the caller that makes it readable.
SELECT a.question AS "question!", a.reason::text AS "reason!", a.times AS "times!"
FROM (
    :compose(reports/absences_in_the_fixtures.sqlc)
) a
ORDER BY a.times DESC, a.question
